[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Common Dynamic Programming Problems: Fibonacci, Knapsack, Longest Common Subsequence"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Dynamic Programming"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dynamic Programming (DP) is an algorithmic technique for solving an optimization problem by breaking it down into simpler subproblems and utilizing the fact that the optimal solution to the overall problem depends upon the optimal solution to its subproblems. DP is applicable when the subproblems are overlapping, meaning that the same subproblems are solved multiple times. By storing the solutions to these subproblems, DP avoids redundant computations, leading to significant efficiency gains."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Fibonacci Sequence"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Fibonacci sequence is a series of numbers where each number is the sum of the two preceding ones, usually starting with 0 and 1. That is, F(0) = 0, F(1) = 1, and F(n) = F(n-1) + F(n-2) for n > 1."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"1.1. Recursive Approach (Inefficient)"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"function fibonacciRecursive(n) {
  if (n <= 1) {
    return n;
  }
  return fibonacciRecursive(n - 1) + fibonacciRecursive(n - 2);
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The recursive approach is simple to understand but highly inefficient due to repeated calculations of the same Fibonacci numbers. Its time complexity is O(2^n)."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"1.2. Dynamic Programming (Memoization)"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"function fibonacciMemoization(n, memo = {}) {
  if (n in memo) {
    return memo[n];
  }
  if (n <= 1) {
    return n;
  }
  memo[n] = fibonacciMemoization(n - 1, memo) + fibonacciMemoization(n - 2, memo);
  return memo[n];
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Memoization is a top-down DP approach where we store the results of expensive function calls and reuse them when the same inputs occur again. This significantly reduces the time complexity to O(n)."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"1.3. Dynamic Programming (Tabulation)"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"function fibonacciTabulation(n) {
  const dp = new Array(n + 1);
  dp[0] = 0;
  dp[1] = 1;
  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i] = dp[i - 1] + dp[i - 2];
  }
  return dp[n];
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Tabulation is a bottom-up DP approach where we build a table of results from the smallest subproblems to the largest. This also has a time complexity of O(n) and can be more efficient than memoization in some cases due to reduced function call overhead."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Knapsack Problem"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The knapsack problem is a classic optimization problem where, given a set of items, each with a weight and a value, we need to determine the most valuable items to include in a knapsack without exceeding its weight capacity."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"2.1. 0/1 Knapsack Problem"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the 0/1 knapsack problem, each item can either be included entirely or excluded (no fractional items). We'll use dynamic programming to solve this."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"function knapsack(capacity, weights, values, n) {
  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill(null).map(() => Array(capacity + 1).fill(0));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let w = 1; w <= capacity; w++) {
      if (weights[i - 1] <= w) {
        dp[i][w] = Math.max(
          values[i - 1] + dp[i - 1][w - weights[i - 1]],
          dp[i - 1][w]
        );
      } else {
        dp[i][w] = dp[i - 1][w];
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[n][capacity];
}"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `knapsack` function uses a 2D array `dp` to store the maximum value that can be obtained with a given capacity and a subset of items. The time complexity is O(n*capacity), where n is the number of items."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) problem involves finding the longest subsequence common to two or more sequences. A subsequence is a sequence that can be derived from another sequence by deleting some or no elements without changing the order of the remaining elements."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"function longestCommonSubsequence(text1, text2) {
  const m = text1.length;
  const n = text2.length;
  const dp = Array(m + 1).fill(null).map(() => Array(n + 1).fill(0));

  for (let i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
      if (text1[i - 1] === text2[j - 1]) {
        dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
      } else {
        dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[m][n];
}"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `longestCommonSubsequence` function uses a 2D array `dp` to store the lengths of the LCS for prefixes of the two input strings. The time complexity is O(m*n), where m and n are the lengths of the input strings."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dynamic Programming is a powerful technique for solving optimization problems by breaking them down into overlapping subproblems. Understanding the core concepts of memoization and tabulation is crucial for effectively applying DP to a wide range of problems. The Fibonacci sequence, knapsack problem, and longest common subsequence are just a few examples of the many problems that can be efficiently solved using DP."}]
